<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1411">1411</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Great War (1409–1411) · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War was fought from 1409 to 1411 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> and the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. Its decisive event, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410, broke the order's military power, though its castle of Marienburg held out; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-peace-of-thorn">First Peace of Thorn</a> (1411) returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> for the lifetimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and Vytautas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-teutonic-war.jpg" alt="Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko,_Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1230 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Kulmerland</a> and, at the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a>, began the Prussian Crusade, conquering the Prussians by the 1280s. They then fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> for about a hundred years, raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>, which separated their lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from their branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>.</p><p>In 1385 Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> agreed in the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kreva">Kreva</a> to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and was crowned King of Poland. Lithuania's conversion removed the religious reason for the Order's campaigns, but the Knights contested its sincerity before a papal court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> had been in Teutonic hands since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-raciaz">Peace of Raciąż</a> of 1404, and the Knights also controlled the lower courses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>An uprising began in Teutonic-held Samogitia in May 1409, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. On 6 August 1409 the Grand Master, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a>, declared war and invaded Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>. Neither side was ready for a full war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus, King of the Romans</a>, mediated a truce signed on 8 October 1409 to expire on 24 June 1410. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and Vytautas agreed to unite their armies and march on Marienburg, and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> at Czerwinsk between 24 and 30 June 1410.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> was fought on 15 July 1410. Estimates give 16,500 to 39,000 Polish–Lithuanian troops and 11,000 to 27,000 Teutonic. About 8,000 Teutonic soldiers were killed and 14,000 captured, and the Grand Master and most of the leadership died. The victors reached Marienburg only on 26 July, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> time to organise a defence. Several cities surrendered, but the siege was lifted on 19 September, and the Knights quickly retook most of their castles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Peace of Thorn was signed on 1 February 1411. The Knights ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and gave up claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> during the lifetimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> and Vytautas. Further wars in 1414 and 1422 preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno">Treaty of Melno</a>, which settled the territorial disputes. The Knights owed an indemnity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, estimated at ten times the annual income of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and met it by borrowing, taxing and confiscating church silver. Danzig and Thorn revolted against the taxes.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Lithuania Christian, the Order struggled to recruit crusaders and relied on costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Unrest led to the Prussian Confederation in 1441 and the Thirteen Years' War in 1454.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
